## Slate 4 — ranked 1 Sep 2026. READ THE EVIDENCE WARNING FIRST

**The remake well is dry.** Ranked from the `--days 28` terms report (4 Aug →
1 Sep, zero ADVERTISING, 78% search). Every large term cluster on the channel
already has a 2026 remake:

| cluster | 28d term views | already covered by |
|---|---:|---|
| magazine | **429** (6 phrasings) | 2025-02 original + **2026-08-20 remake** |
| book cover | **305** (9 phrasings) | 2024-11 + 2026-08 KDP cover |
| draw / digital art | 150 | 2025-01 + 2026-08 draw tool |
| menu | 71 | 2024-09 (123k) + **2026-08-08 remake** |
| labels | 44 | three versions — **do NOT redo** |
| report · wallpaper · pitch deck | 32 · 26 · 21 | all remade or scheduled |

Magazine looked like the obvious #4 — biggest cluster by 1.4× — until the
catalogue check found the 20 Aug 2026 remake. A third magazine video would be
the product-labels mistake again. **Always grep the catalogue before ranking a
term.**

### ⚠ Evidence warning — slate 4 is a weaker class than slate 3

The terms report can only show queries **we already rank for**. It is
structurally blind to uncovered demand. Every slate-3 row carried a measured
view count; **no slate-4 row does**, because by definition there is no video to
measure. These rows are ranked on catalogue absence + funnel strength, which is
judgement, not measurement. Treat as a queue, not a commitment, and get outside
keyword volume before committing to any of them.

### The ranking lens changed

Ranked on **which existing pack the video sells**, not on topic variety. The
"vary the topic so the channel isn't one-note" note on slate 3 was aimed at
returning viewers — and this channel does not have returning viewers, it has
search traffic where every video is a separate front door. The real reason to
vary is **pack cannibalisation**: buyers only need one print pack. So rows that
sell an ALREADY-BUILT pack rank highest, because they are revenue with no build
cost, and speed to revenue is a hard constraint.

| # | Title (search query) | Why | Pack |
|---|---|---|---|
| 4 | **Colouring Book / Colouring Pages in Canva for KDP** | **Zero coverage in 417 videos.** Big, well-established KDP income niche; tier-1 US/UK sellers | `kdp-interior-pack` — **already built**, pure upsell |
| 5 | **Wedding Stationery — invitations + on-the-day set** | 2 videos, none since 2024. Higher-value niche than baby showers, same method | `party-printables-kit` — **already built** and literally covers it |
| 6 | **Workbook / Digital Workbook** | Zero coverage. Doubles as teacher resource and creator lead magnet | `tpt-worksheet-pack` — **already built** |
| 7 | **Sticker Sheets for Etsy** | 3 videos, none since 2025. Strong Etsy seller intent | none — needs building |

**CUT: Resume / CV.** It failed the channel's own tier-1 filter and should never
have been listed. Resume/CV is a globally-searched query that pulls exactly the
India/Pakistan/Philippines traffic the turnaround was built to escape — it would
push the RPM back toward £0.98. It got in because it was ranked on search
breadth. **Every row must pass the filter first: does only a US/UK BUYER search
this?** KDP, Etsy, TPT and small-business documents pass. Generic career,
student and social-media queries do not.

### 🔒 FROZEN until after 5 Oct 2026

Slate 4 is a queue, not a plan, and it is **closed to re-ranking**. Slates 3 and
4 were both ranked on 1 Sep, hours apart, and the second overrode a note from
the first — that is churn, and it costs more than it finds. Nothing new can be
learned until the 21 Sep – 5 Oct publications have run and the format verdict is
in. Do not re-rank on a fresh terms report before then, and do not re-open this
table because a new number appeared.

Dropped from consideration: **web banners** (481 views but NOT ONE banner term
in the top 25 — the views are long-tail, and buyer intent is weak) and
**infographics** (279 views, and already two videos: 2024-08 and 2025-09).

Re-rank after 5 Oct, when the 21 Sep–5 Oct publications give the first read on
whether the code-rendered format wins.

### Logged for the 5 Oct checkpoint — do NOT act on before then

RPM sheet analysed 1 Sep 2026 (`RPM SHEET - Ranked by Highest RPM.xlsx`).

**Cohort RPM, aggregated** — the only honest way to read RPM, because individual
rows have too few views to mean anything:

| cohort | videos | views | revenue | RPM |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 2024 | 93 | 28,667 | £27.23 | £0.95 |
| 2025 | 37 | 11,815 | £15.33 | £1.30 |
| **2026** | 28 | 1,224 | £6.67 | **£5.45** |

The 2026 cohort earns **5.7× per view** what the 2024 cohort does. That is the
tier-1 query strategy working, and it is the strongest evidence yet FOR the
current anchor — not a reason to change it.

**The eye-catching per-video RPMs are noise.** Priced in real money: Canva AI
Workflow £32.94 RPM × 18 views = **53p**; Pet Tags £29.22 × 35 = **80p**;
Planners & Journals £22.00 × 2 views = **2p**; Media Kit £17.86 × 23 = **13p**.
All the giant-RPM videos together earn less than the menu video alone (£6.03).
**Never rank on an RPM below ~500 views** — at 2 views one ad impression makes
any RPM you like.

**Print-fix hypothesis — CLOSED 1 Sep 2026, answered NO.** I logged it as "watch
in October", then checked the whole cluster instead of the four I had seen. The
channel has ALREADY made **17** print-fix / troubleshooting videos:

| | videos | views | views/video | revenue | RPM |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| print-fix / troubleshooting | 17 | 764 | **45** | £4.39 | **£5.75** |
| everything else | 141 | 40,943 | **290** | £44.84 | £1.10 |

The RPM lift is real (5.2×, across 17 videos — not a small-sample fluke). It
just does not survive the traffic gap: **5.2× RPM × ⅙ the views = 0.8× the
revenue per video.** More of them would earn LESS than a normal tutorial. And
56% of that cluster's revenue is one video (cropped-when-printed, £2.45); strip
it and cluster RPM falls to £3.16.

**Why:** troubleshooting is a low-volume query. People search "how to make a menu
in canva", hit the problem days later, and never search for the fix. You reach
them by being INSIDE the tutorial they searched for — not with a video about the
problem.

**So print-fix is a COMPONENT, not a format**, and it is already deployed
correctly: the S06 trap in every tutorial, the Shorts cut from that trap, and the
print checklist lead magnet. Do not commission standalone print-fix long-forms.

**Spreadsheet defect:** 34 rows carry the STRING `"10"` in both revenue and RPM.
That is YouTube's below-threshold placeholder, not £10. Counting it inflates
total revenue from £49.23 to £389.23 — 8×. Filter non-numeric cells before any
sort or average on that file.
